module gunbc.tools.body_lowering_population_probe

import gunbc.tools.frontier_ingestion_probe {
  CensusRefusedAtReconciliation,
  CensusRefusedAtWorld,
  CensusTaken,
  FrontendCensusReceipt,
  FrontierMember,
  FrontendStage,
  LexRefused,
  MemberObservation,
  NormalizeAccepted,
  NormalizeGraftRefused,
  NormalizeOther,
  NormalizeRetained,
  ParseRefused,
  take_census_over
}
import v2.std.algebra { fold_list }
import v2.std.collection { List }
import v2.std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, ReadsLiveTree }
import v2.std.compilers.lexing { symbol_lexeme }
import v2.std.node { Symbol }
import v2.std.text { String }

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = ReadsLiveTree

data body_lowering_population_probe_note: String = "BL-2 REPRODUCTION INSTRUMENT, homed outside a *_test.dag module for the same reason gunbc.tools.frontier_ingestion_probe is: what it reports is a revision-scoped OBSERVATION of the present frontier, and enrolling that as a test declaration would make an assertion that goes RED on the repair and GREEN on a regression. It reuses take_census_over and classify_source rather than reproducing them, so there is one stage classifier in the repository and not two.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS. The body-lowering wall cannot be scheduled against an unmeasured population. normalize today returns Accepted for a tree whose diagnostics carry wrapper-retention, and resolve consumes it, so retention is kept out of resolve by propagation rather than by construction. Making retention unconstructible is the right wall and it CHANGES WHICH MODULES NORMALIZE ACCEPTS — so the population that would newly refuse has to be known before the wall lands, not discovered from a red tree afterwards.\n\nWHAT THIS ROSTER IS. The body-lowering subjects: the two SH-D shards with measured door receipts, the four std members the pre-repair frontier observation recorded as retained plus the one it never re-ran, and the bare-expression specimens v2.lens.vacuity independently found rejecting at this seam. It is deliberately NOT the whole corpus — a per-file census over every module is a different and much larger instrument — so a count taken here is a count over THESE members and carries no claim about the rest.\n\nWHAT A READER MAY NOT DO WITH IT. The per-member stage is the observation. There is no aggregate pass/fail, because the population is the finding and a Boolean over it would be a verdict on subjects that have not all been separately established."

data body_lowering_population_first_reading_note: String = "FIRST EXECUTED READING (2026-08-13, three-member roster): use_site_verdict NORM_ACCEPTED, materialization_carriers NORM_OTHER, optional NORM_RETAINED.\n\nWHAT IT CHANGES, and it is the shard rows rather than the finding. Neither SH-D shard RETAINS as a file. use_site_verdict normalizes clean; materialization_carriers refuses at normalize for something that is neither retention nor a graft refusal. So the wrapper-retained residue measured in those shards' door chains does not come from the shard modules themselves — it comes from OTHER modules in their import closures, of which optional is one confirmed instance. Saying a shard is body-lowering blocked was too coarse: the shard is blocked because its CLOSURE contains retaining modules, and the subjects to repair are those, not it.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. Three members are not a population, and NORM_OTHER is a cause this instrument does not identify — it names the stage, not the reason. materialization_carriers refusing at normalize for an unidentified reason is an open question, not a measured cause, and nothing here establishes which closure members of either shard retain beyond the one measured.\n\nA COST OBSERVATION, recorded because it bounds what can be asked next: the same census over ten members ran 2h51m without a verdict; over three it returns in about two minutes. That is nonlinear in the member count, so one or more dropped members is pathological rather than merely larger. WHICH member is NOT established — no per-member timing was taken — and attributing it to the largest file would be a guess dressed as a finding."

data body_lowering_population_roster_cost_note: String = "THE ROSTER IS THREE, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. A ten-member roster over these subjects was executed and ABANDONED at 2h51m wall and 8.3 GiB resident without producing a verdict — on a host whose shared slice is 20 GiB and whose kernel kills the LARGEST task in it, so that run was a hazard to every other session before it was a slow measurement. The instrument this extends already declared the cost: its own roster note records the accepted-classification path measuring over the five-second fast-lane budget for fifteen files, and states that the batched or realized execution which would make a live census affordable is still owed. So the roster is sized to what the present instrument can deliver, not to what the question deserves, and that gap is a deficit in the instrument rather than a property of the subjects.\n\nWHAT THE THREE ARE: the two SH-D shards with measured door receipts, plus one std member the pre-repair observation recorded as retained. THE SEVEN DROPPED MEMBERS ARE NOT OBSERVED-AND-EXCLUDED — they are unmeasured, and no count taken here may be read as covering them. Dissolve-on: the realized census execution the instrument's own note owes, at which point the roster returns to the full population in one run."

data body_lowering_population_roster: List<FrontierMember> = [
  FrontierMember { label: ^use_site_verdict, path: "src/v2/compiler/use_site_verdict.dag" },
  FrontierMember { label: ^materialization_carriers, path: "src/v2/compiler/materialization_carriers.dag" },
  FrontierMember { label: ^optional, path: "src/v2/std/optional.dag" },
]

fn take_body_lowering_population_census() -> FrontendCensusReceipt {
  take_census_over(rows: body_lowering_population_roster)
}

fn stage_label_of(s: FrontendStage) -> String {
  match s {
    LexRefused => "LEX"
    ParseRefused => "PARSE"
    NormalizeGraftRefused => "NORM_GRAFT_REFUSED"
    NormalizeRetained => "NORM_RETAINED"
    NormalizeOther => "NORM_OTHER"
    NormalizeAccepted => "NORM_ACCEPTED"
  }
}

fn observation_line(o: MemberObservation) -> String {
  concat(symbol_lexeme(sym: o.label), concat(" ", stage_label_of(s: o.stage)))
}

fn census_report() -> String {
  match take_body_lowering_population_census() {
    CensusRefusedAtWorld => "REFUSED_AT_WORLD"
    CensusRefusedAtReconciliation { cause: _ } => "REFUSED_AT_RECONCILIATION"
    CensusTaken { roster_identity: _, observations: obs } =>
      fold_list(
        xs: obs,
        empty: "",
        cons: fn(acc, o) {
          if acc == "" { observation_line(o: o) } else { concat(acc, concat(" | ", observation_line(o: o))) }
        }
      )
  }
}

data body_lowering_no_aggregate_note: String = "THERE IS NO AGGREGATE COUNT HERE, and its absence is the module's own stated scope rather than an omission. An earlier revision carried count_stage_matching and retained_member_count, and both were deleted for defects worth recording so they are not reintroduced.\n\nA REFUSAL BECAME AN INTEGER. count_stage_matching answered 0 - 1 for CensusRefusedAtWorld and 0 - 2 for CensusRefusedAtReconciliation, so a typed refusal and a genuine population count inhabited one type. A caller could add, compare or display a failure as a measurement, and -1 is a plausible-looking number rather than a loud one. The census already carries typed refusal arms; collapsing them into the counting type discarded exactly the distinction those arms exist to preserve.\n\nPRESENTATION BECAME SEMANTIC AUTHORITY. It counted by comparing stage_label_of(s) against a target String. FrontendStage is a closed coproduct, so a label rename would have changed the count with no stage changing, and a label collision would have merged distinct stages — the renderer deciding a semantic question. Counting belongs to the structural variant or to frontend_stage_eq, never to rendered text.\n\nBoth were reachable because an aggregate was authored at all. This module's note already says the per-member observation IS the finding and that no aggregate verdict is warranted over a roster whose members have not each been separately established; the helpers contradicted that. A reader wanting a count over these members reads the per-member observations and counts the subjects it can name."
